Part A — Creating Data Frames
Question #1

a. Create a vector.
```{r}
scores <- c(Ana = 78, Ben = 92, Cid = 85, Dex = 92, Eli = 67, Fay = 88, Gio = 74)
scores
```
b. Sort the scores in ascending order and descending order. What is the output?

```{r}
# Ascending order
sort(scores)

# Descending order
sort(scores, decreasing = TRUE)
```
    The output for the ascending order is Eli (67), Gio (74), Ana (78), Cid (85), Fay (88), Ben (92), Dex (92) while the output for the descending order is Ben (92), Dex (92), Fay (88), Cid (85), Ana (78), Gio (74), Eli (67).
    
c. Print your sorted result from sort-ascending. Check whether the # names (“Ana”, “Ben”, …) stayed attached to their correct values.
```{r}
sorted_scores <- sort(scores)
sorted_scores
```
    Yes, the names stayed attached to their respective scores because it is already assigned to them directly.
    
d. Create another vectors:
subjects <- c("Science", "Math", "English", "Filipino", "PE") incomplete <- c(85, NA, 90, 70, NA, 95)
```{r}
subjects <- c("Science", "Math", "English", "Filipino", "PE")
incomplete <- c(85, NA, 90, 70, NA, 95)
```

e. Sort subjects alphabetically using sort()
```{r}
sort(subjects)
```
    The output is "English","Filipino","Math","PE","Science".
    
f. Sort incomplete using sort(). Observe what happens to the NA
```{r}
# Default behavior (removes NA)
sort(incomplete)

# Keeping NA at the end
sort(incomplete, na.last = TRUE)
```
    By default, sort() completely removes the NA values. When using na.last = TRUE, the numbers are sorted first, and the NA values are placed at the end of the vector.
    
Question #2:

* When you sorted scores, did the names stay correctly attached to their original values?
* Explain why this matters: if you were sorting exam scores to find the top performer, what would go wrong if the names did NOT follow their values?

    Yes, the names stayed correctly attached to their original values because the names were part of the vector attributes.
    If names didn't follow the values, the highest score would end up matched with the wrong student, giving credit to someone who didn't actually score it.

Question #3:

* By default, sort() silently removes NA values instead of showing them or throwing an error.
* Why might this default behavior be dangerous if you were reporting “the lowest score” in a dataset with missing data, without first checking for NA values?.

    This default behavior might be dangerous because removing NA values also removes/hides missing data. You might report a low score without realizing that some students missed the exam completely or haven't had their grades recorded yet.
    
